---
Part X, Chapter 4: The chrome every page wears
Package: site/src/
Language: TypeScript
---

# The chrome every page wears

Every page of the site opens with the same nav and closes on the same footer, and a slash typed anywhere raises the same search palette over the top. None of it belongs to a page, so all of it lives here.

The chrome needs to know what the site contains. That knowledge is one list of pages, written once and read three times over: the nav marks the current page, the palette groups its results under the page each one came from, and the footer links out. A page added to that list appears in all three.

The chrome draws its markup with a builder it does not own. Foldkit hands the site's root view a builder named `h`, typed to the site's messages, and no module can make one for itself. So every function here that draws markup takes `h` as its last argument, and the view passes its own builder down to each piece of chrome it renders. The marks and icons borrowed from the components carry no handlers, so they draw themselves and take no builder at all.

## The pages the nav names

Four pages sit in the nav, in the order a reader meets them: the docs and the community either side of the wordmark, then the argument for Loom and the source it is written in. Each carries its own woven mark, which the palette shows beside its name.

The nav's own labels are lower case, because the whole row is set in capitals by the stylesheet and a capitalised label would come out shouting.

=>

import { Array, Match, Option, pipe } from 'effect'
import { inertHtml as ih, type Html, type HtmlBuilder } from 'foldkit/html'
import {
  ClosedSearch,
  MovedFocus,
  OpenedDrawer,
  OpenedSearch,
  Typed,
  WentTo,
  type Message,
  type Model,
  type Route,
} from './model'
import {
  broadSeam,
  burgerIcon,
  carpetMark,
  communityCarpet,
  exampleCarpet,
  docsCarpet,
  loomMark,
  octocatIcon,
  sourceCarpet,
  whyLoomCarpet,
  type Carpet,
} from './components'

export type NavPage = {
  readonly route: Route
  readonly label: string
  readonly href: string
  readonly carpet: Carpet
}

export const navPages: ReadonlyArray<NavPage> = [
  { route: 'docs', label: 'docs', href: '/docs', carpet: docsCarpet },
  { route: 'community', label: 'community', href: '/community', carpet: communityCarpet },
  { route: 'why-loom', label: 'why loom', href: '/why-loom', carpet: whyLoomCarpet },
  { route: 'source', label: 'source', href: '/source', carpet: sourceCarpet },
]

## The nav across the top

The nav is a row of links with the wordmark set into the middle of it, so the site's name reads as part of the navigation rather than as a badge pinned beside it. The two pages before the wordmark and the two after it come from the same list, split in half.

Search closes the row. It says `search` on a wide window and `navs` on a narrow one, where it has taken over from the links entirely, and the key badge beside it shows the slash that opens it.

=>

const navLink = (page: NavPage, here: boolean, h: HtmlBuilder<Message>): Html =>
  h.a(
    [
      h.Class(here ? 'nav-link here' : 'nav-link'),
      h.Href(page.href),
      h.OnClick(WentTo({ route: page.route })),
    ],
    [page.label],
  )

export const nav = (model: Model, h: HtmlBuilder<Message>): Html =>
  h.nav(
    [h.Class('nav'), h.Role('navigation')],
    [
      ...Array.map(Array.take(navPages, 2), (page) => navLink(page, page.route === model.route, h)),
      h.a(
        [
          h.Class('nav-word'),
          h.Href('/'),
          h.AriaLabel('Loom'),
          h.OnClick(WentTo({ route: 'home' })),
        ],
        ['Loom'],
      ),
      ...Array.map(Array.drop(navPages, 2), (page) => navLink(page, page.route === model.route, h)),
      findButton('nav-find', 'Search Loom — press /', h),
    ],
  )

## The button that opens the palette

Both bars carry the same search button, and it is the same markup in each — a word, and a key cap showing the slash that opens the palette. Only where it sits differs: the marketing bar pins it to the right edge so the links stay centred, while the documentation bar lets it sit in a row at the end. The class decides that, and the button itself does not know which bar it is in.

=>

const findButton = (className: string, title: string, h: HtmlBuilder<Message>): Html =>
  h.button(
    [
      h.Class(className),
      h.Type('button'),
      h.AriaLabel('Navigate and search'),
      h.Title(title),
      h.OnClick(OpenedSearch()),
    ],
    [
      h.span([h.Class('wide')], ['search']),
      h.span([h.Class('narrow')], ['navs']),
      h.span([h.Class('nav-key'), h.AriaHidden(true)], ['/']),
    ],
  )

## The documentation's own bar

The documentation does not wear the marketing bar. It reads like an editor, so its bar behaves like one: the wordmark sits hard left rather than centred, the bar sticks to the top of the window with a rule beneath it, and it slides out of the way as a reader scrolls down and returns the moment they scroll back.

The wordmark is larger here than in the marketing bar — twenty-four pixels against nineteen — because it is no longer competing with links on both sides for the middle of the row.

At the right end sit the search button and a link to the repository, and that link names the version the server read from npm rather than a number written into the page.

Below a thousand pixels the links give way to a burger, which opens the drawer that carries the rail.

=>

const REPO = 'https://github.com/athrio-com/loom'

export const docsBar = (model: Model, h: HtmlBuilder<Message>): Html =>
  h.div(
    [h.Class(model.navHidden ? 'docs-bar hidden' : 'docs-bar')],
    [
      h.button(
        [
          h.Class('bar-burger'),
          h.Type('button'),
          h.Title('Menu'),
          h.AriaLabel('Menu'),
          h.OnClick(OpenedDrawer()),
        ],
        [burgerIcon()],
      ),
      h.a(
        [h.Class('bar-word'), h.Href('/'), h.AriaLabel('Loom'), h.OnClick(WentTo({ route: 'home' }))],
        [loomMark('paper', 0.495), h.span([h.Class('bar-word-text')], ['Loom'])],
      ),
      h.div(
        [h.Class('bar-links')],
        Array.map(navPages, (page) => navLink(page, page.route === model.route, h)),
      ),
      h.div(
        [h.Class('bar-end')],
        [
          findButton('bar-find', 'Search the docs — press /', h),
          h.a(
            [h.Class('bar-repo'), h.Href(REPO), h.Title('View the repo on GitHub')],
            [octocatIcon('15'), h.span([], [`v${model.version}`])],
          ),
        ],
      ),
    ],
  )

## The pager that names its neighbours

Every page ends by naming the two either side of it. The site has a reading order — the docs, the community, Loom itself, the argument, then the source — and the pager is that order made walkable, so a reader who finishes a page is never left at a dead end.

A card is only its mark and two lines of type. Under the cursor those two lines take the colour of the page they lead to, so the card names its destination twice over — once in words and once in ink. That colour is the destination's own hue deepened until it holds against paper, since the bright value each page wears on a dark card would read as a highlighter here.

The first page has nothing before it and the last nothing after, so a pager can carry one card rather than two. The card that remains keeps its side of the row.

=>

type Neighbour = {
  readonly route: Route
  readonly label: string
  readonly href: string
  readonly carpet: Carpet
  readonly ink: string
}

const readingOrder: ReadonlyArray<Neighbour> = [
  {
    route: 'docs',
    label: 'Docs',
    href: '/docs',
    carpet: docsCarpet,
    ink: '#1D4FBF',
  },
  {
    route: 'community',
    label: 'Community',
    href: '/community',
    carpet: communityCarpet,
    ink: '#6D3BD4',
  },
  {
    route: 'home',
    label: 'Loom',
    href: '/',
    carpet: exampleCarpet,
    ink: '#00714F',
  },
  {
    route: 'why-loom',
    label: 'Why Loom',
    href: '/why-loom',
    carpet: whyLoomCarpet,
    ink: '#C08A00',
  },
  {
    route: 'source',
    label: 'Source',
    href: '/source',
    carpet: sourceCarpet,
    ink: '#C2410C',
  },
]

~

A card is the mark, then the eyebrow that says which direction it goes, then the page's name. The next card mirrors it: the text ranges right and the mark sits after it, so the two cards read outward from the middle of the row rather than both pointing the same way.

The hover tint arrives as a pair of custom properties on the card, because a hover cannot be written inline — the rule that uses them lives in the stylesheet and the values belong to the destination.

=>

const pagerCard = (
  page: Neighbour,
  direction: 'Previous' | 'Next',
  h: HtmlBuilder<Message>,
): Html => {
  const mark = carpetMark(page.carpet, '42px', broadSeam)
  const words = h.div(
    [h.Class('pager-words')],
    [
      h.span([h.Class('eyebrow')], [direction]),
      h.span([h.Class('pager-title')], [page.label]),
    ],
  )
  return h.a(
    [
      h.Class(direction === 'Next' ? 'pager-card next' : 'pager-card'),
      h.Href(page.href),
      h.Style({ '--pg-ink': page.ink }),
      h.OnClick(WentTo({ route: page.route })),
    ],
    direction === 'Next' ? [words, mark] : [mark, words],
  )
}

export const pager = (route: Route, h: HtmlBuilder<Message>): Html => {
  const at = Array.findFirstIndex(readingOrder, (page) => page.route === route)
  const step = (by: number): Option.Option<Neighbour> =>
    pipe(
      at,
      Option.flatMap((index) => Option.fromNullishOr(readingOrder[index + by])),
    )
  return h.div(
    [h.Class('pager'), h.Id('loom-pager')],
    [
      h.div(
        [h.Class('pager-row')],
        [
          ...Option.toArray(Option.map(step(-1), (page) => pagerCard(page, 'Previous', h))),
          ...Option.toArray(Option.map(step(1), (page) => pagerCard(page, 'Next', h))),
        ],
      ),
    ],
  )
}

~

The documentation ends on a plainer pager. It sits inside the article's own column rather than across the page, so it has no room for a forty-two-pixel mark and does not try to find one — two cards of equal width, a name in each, and a ground that lifts on hover rather than taking on the destination's colour.

Dropping the hue here is deliberate. On a marketing page the tint previews where a card goes; in the documentation a reader is moving between chapters of one thing, and five colours down the bottom of every page would be noise.

=>

const docsPagerCard = (
  page: Neighbour,
  direction: 'Previous' | 'Next',
  h: HtmlBuilder<Message>,
): Html =>
  h.a(
    [
      h.Class(direction === 'Next' ? 'docs-pager-card next' : 'docs-pager-card'),
      h.Href(page.href),
      h.OnClick(WentTo({ route: page.route })),
    ],
    [
      h.span([h.Class('eyebrow')], [direction]),
      h.span([h.Class('pager-title')], [page.label]),
    ],
  )

export const docsPager = (route: Route, h: HtmlBuilder<Message>): Html => {
  const at = Array.findFirstIndex(readingOrder, (page) => page.route === route)
  const step = (by: number): Option.Option<Neighbour> =>
    pipe(
      at,
      Option.flatMap((index) => Option.fromNullishOr(readingOrder[index + by])),
    )
  return h.div(
    [h.Class('docs-pager')],
    [
      ...Option.toArray(Option.map(step(-1), (page) => docsPagerCard(page, 'Previous', h))),
      ...Option.toArray(Option.map(step(1), (page) => docsPagerCard(page, 'Next', h))),
    ],
  )
}

## What the palette searches

The palette searches the site's prose, not a list of page titles. Each page contributes the passages inside it, so a reader who half-remembers a sentence finds the page that says it — and because every mark and every command is named in that prose, the marks are findable through it without an index of their own.

A page carries the colour the palette wears while its results are in focus. These are the palette's own inks, lifted for a dark ground, so they run brighter than the same page's woven mark.

=>

export type SearchPage = {
  readonly route: Route
  readonly label: string
  readonly path: string
  readonly ink: string
  readonly rgb: string
  readonly text: string
}

export const searchPages: ReadonlyArray<SearchPage> = [
  {
    route: 'docs',
    label: 'Docs',
    path: '/docs',
    ink: '#6E9BFF',
    rgb: '110, 155, 255',
    text: 'The eight marks, and what tangling does with them.',
  },
  {
    route: 'why-loom',
    label: 'Why Loom',
    path: '/why-loom',
    ink: '#FF6A3D',
    rgb: '255, 106, 61',
    text: 'The case for writing the document first.',
  },
  {
    route: 'community',
    label: 'Community',
    path: '/community',
    ink: '#F45C9F',
    rgb: '244, 92, 159',
    text: 'People, help and the changelog.',
  },
  {
    route: 'home',
    label: 'Loom',
    path: '/',
    ink: '#F2EFE6',
    rgb: '242, 239, 230',
    text: 'What Loom is, in one screen.',
  },
]

~

Each passage names the page it sits on, its own heading, and the sentence a reader might recall. The docs passages describe the marks as Loom writes them today: a `---` fence around the frontmatter, a `#` heading naming a section, a `[file.ts]` sink naming the file a section's code belongs to, an anchor drawing another section in whole, and the `=>` and `~` operators opening and closing code.

One of those marks cannot be written down here. Loom recognises an anchor by its opening glyphs wherever they fall inside a code block — a string is no exception — so a sentence that spells one out would be read as a real anchor and tangling would fail looking for the section it seems to name. A passage that shows a reader an anchor therefore assembles the mark from pieces, and the site is left able to quote the language it is written in.

=>

export type Passage = {
  readonly route: Route
  readonly title: string
  readonly text: string
}

export const anchorMark = (name: string): string => `${':'}:[${name}]`

export const passages: ReadonlyArray<Passage> = [
  {
    route: 'docs',
    title: 'The eight marks',
    text: `Dashes --- fence the frontmatter, a # heading names a section, brackets [file.ts] name the file its code belongs to, a brace label says what Loom makes of a heading, and an anchor ${anchorMark('a section')} draws another section in whole.`,
