## A fragment, windowed on the match

A result shows the passage's own words rather than its heading, windowed so the match sits inside what a reader can see. Sixty-two characters is the window, the same measure a run of prose takes on the page.

The window opens twenty-two characters ahead of the match and then slides forward to the next word break, so it starts on a word rather than mid-syllable. An ellipsis marks each end that was cut. Whatever space the match and its lead-in leave over goes to the text after it.

=>

export type Fragment = {
  readonly pre: string
  readonly hit: string
  readonly post: string
}

const WINDOW = 62
const LEAD_IN = 22

const toWordBreak = (text: string): string => text.replace(/\s+\S*$/, '')

const clipped = (text: string): string =>
  text.length > WINDOW ? `${toWordBreak(text.slice(0, WINDOW))}…` : text

const whole = (text: string): Fragment => ({ pre: clipped(text), hit: '', post: '' })

~

Finding the match is its own small question, and it answers with an `Option` so that "nowhere in this text" stays a value rather than a magic index.

=>

const indexOfIgnoringCase = (haystack: string, needle: string): Option.Option<number> => {
  const at = haystack.toLowerCase().indexOf(needle.toLowerCase())
  return at < 0 ? Option.none() : Option.some(at)
}

const windowedAt = (source: string, at: number, length: number): Fragment => {
  const from = Math.max(0, at - LEAD_IN)
  const space = source.indexOf(' ', from)
  const start = from > 0 && space >= 0 && space < at ? space + 1 : from
  const pre = `${start > 0 ? '…' : ''}${source.slice(start, at)}`
  const hit = source.slice(at, at + length)
  const room = Math.max(0, WINDOW - pre.length - hit.length)
  const rest = source.slice(at + length)
  const post = rest.length > room ? `${toWordBreak(rest.slice(0, room))}…` : rest
  return { pre, hit, post }
}

~

The passage's own sentence is tried first. A query that matches only the heading falls back to the heading and the sentence read as one line, so the match still shows somewhere. A query that matches neither leaves the sentence to stand on its own, clipped to the window.

=>

export const fragmentOf = (passage: Passage, query: string): Fragment => {
  const needle = query.trim()
  const titled = `${passage.title} · ${passage.text}`
  return needle === ''
    ? whole(passage.text)
    : pipe(
        indexOfIgnoringCase(passage.text, needle),
        Option.map((at) => windowedAt(passage.text, at, needle.length)),
        Option.orElse(() =>
          pipe(
            indexOfIgnoringCase(titled, needle),
            Option.map((at) => windowedAt(titled, at, needle.length)),
          ),
        ),
        Option.getOrElse(() => whole(passage.text)),
      )
}

## The results, grouped under their page

A query gathers into groups, one per page, and a page keeps at most three of its passages so no single page can fill the list. A page appears when one of its passages matches, and also when the page's own name or path does — a reader typing `docs` wants the page itself.

=>

export type Group = {
  readonly page: SearchPage
  readonly rows: ReadonlyArray<Passage>
}

const holds = (needle: string, text: string): boolean => text.toLowerCase().includes(needle)

export const groupsFor = (query: string): ReadonlyArray<Group> => {
  const needle = query.trim().toLowerCase()
  return needle === ''
    ? []
    : pipe(
        searchPages,
        Array.map((page) => ({
          page,
          rows: pipe(
            passages,
            Array.filter(
              (passage) =>
                passage.route === page.route &&
                holds(needle, `${passage.title} ${passage.text}`),
            ),
            Array.take(3),
          ),
        })),
        Array.filter(
          (group) =>
            group.rows.length > 0 ||
            holds(needle, `${group.page.label} ${group.page.text} ${group.page.path}`),
        ),
      )
}

~

The arrow keys walk one flat list, even though the palette draws two levels. Flattening the groups — each page followed by its own passages — is what lets a single focus number address either kind of row.

=>

export type Focusable =
  | { readonly kind: 'page'; readonly page: SearchPage }
  | { readonly kind: 'passage'; readonly page: SearchPage; readonly passage: Passage }

export const focusables = (groups: ReadonlyArray<Group>): ReadonlyArray<Focusable> =>
  pipe(
    groups,
    Array.flatMap((group) => [
      { kind: 'page' as const, page: group.page },
      ...Array.map(group.rows, (passage) => ({
        kind: 'passage' as const,
        page: group.page,
        passage,
      })),
    ]),
  )

const focusedIn = (list: ReadonlyArray<Focusable>, focus: number): Option.Option<Focusable> =>
  Option.fromNullishOr(list[Math.min(focus, Math.max(0, list.length - 1))])

## The keys the palette answers to

Escape closes the palette. The arrows move the focus within however many rows the query produced, and Enter follows whichever row holds it. Every other key belongs to the input, so the palette answers it by moving the focus nowhere.

=>

const paletteKey = (key: string, model: Model): Message => {
  const list = focusables(groupsFor(model.query))
  const stay = MovedFocus({ delta: 0, count: list.length })
  return Match.value(key).pipe(
    Match.when('Escape', () => ClosedSearch()),
    Match.when('ArrowDown', () => MovedFocus({ delta: 1, count: list.length })),
    Match.when('ArrowUp', () => MovedFocus({ delta: -1, count: list.length })),
    Match.when('Enter', () =>
      pipe(
        focusedIn(list, model.focus),
        Option.match({
          onNone: () => stay,
          onSome: (focusable) => WentTo({ route: focusable.page.route }),
        }),
      ),
    ),
    Match.orElse(() => stay),
  )
}

## The query and its own caret

The input holds the query, and a mirror of that query sits behind it carrying the caret. The mirror is transparent apart from a white block at the caret's position, showing the character underneath it in navy so the letter stays readable inside the block.

An empty query indents its placeholder clear of the block, which otherwise sits over the first word of it.

=>

const caretMirror = (model: Model, h: HtmlBuilder<Message>): Html => {
  const at = Math.min(model.caret, model.query.length)
  return h.span(
    [h.Class('palette-mirror'), h.AriaHidden(true)],
    [
      model.query.slice(0, at),
      h.span([h.Class('palette-caret')], [model.query.charAt(at)]),
    ],
  )
}

const queryField = (model: Model, h: HtmlBuilder<Message>): Html =>
  h.span(
    [h.Class('palette-field')],
    [
      h.input([
        h.Class('palette-input'),
        h.Value(model.query),
        h.Placeholder('Go to a page, or search'),
        h.Spellcheck(false),
        h.Style({ textIndent: model.query === '' ? '13px' : '0' }),
        h.OnInput((query) => Typed({ query })),
        h.OnKeyDown((key) => paletteKey(key, model)),
      ]),
      caretMirror(model, h),
    ],
  )

## The pills, when nothing is typed

With an empty query the palette offers the site itself rather than an empty list. Four pills name the pages, each behind its woven mark, and two more name the places Loom lives away from its own site. Each pill pops in a beat after the one before, so the row assembles in front of a reader instead of appearing whole.

The pills are fixed when the module loads, before any view has a builder to lend. A brand mark answers no click, so it draws with Foldkit's inert builder, `ih`, which builds markup that can never send a message.

=>

type Pill = {
  readonly label: string
  readonly href: string
  readonly mark: Html
}

const brandMark = (viewBox: string, fill: string, path: string): Html =>
  ih.span(
    [
      ih.AriaHidden(true),
      ih.Style({ display: 'block', flex: 'none', width: '17px', height: '17px' }),
      ih.InnerHTML(
        `<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="${viewBox}" fill="${fill}" width="17" height="17"><path d="${path}"/></svg>`,
      ),
    ],
    [],
  )

const GITHUB_PATH =
  'M12 .5C5.7.5.6 5.6.6 12c0 5 3.3 9.3 7.8 10.8.6.1.8-.2.8-.6v-2c-3.2.7-3.9-1.5-3.9-1.5-.5-1.3-1.3-1.7-1.3-1.7-1-.7.1-.7.1-.7 1.1.1 1.7 1.2 1.7 1.2 1 1.8 2.7 1.3 3.4 1 .1-.8.4-1.3.7-1.6-2.6-.3-5.3-1.3-5.3-5.8 0-1.3.4-2.3 1.2-3.1-.1-.3-.5-1.5.1-3.1 0 0 1-.3 3.2 1.2.9-.3 1.9-.4 2.9-.4s2 .1 2.9.4c2.2-1.5 3.2-1.2 3.2-1.2.6 1.6.2 2.8.1 3.1.8.8 1.2 1.8 1.2 3.1 0 4.5-2.7 5.5-5.3 5.8.4.4.8 1.1.8 2.2v3.3c0 .4.2.7.8.6 4.5-1.5 7.8-5.8 7.8-10.8C23.4 5.6 18.3.5 12 .5z'

const DISCORD_PATH =
  'M60.1 4.9A58.5 58.5 0 0 0 45.6.4a.2.2 0 0 0-.2.1c-.6 1.1-1.3 2.6-1.8 3.7a54 54 0 0 0-16.2 0c-.5-1.2-1.2-2.6-1.9-3.7a.2.2 0 0 0-.2-.1c-5 .9-9.9 2.4-14.5 4.5a.2.2 0 0 0-.1.1C1.6 18.7-.9 32.1.3 45.4c0 .1 0 .2.1.2a58.9 58.9 0 0 0 17.8 9 .2.2 0 0 0 .2-.1c1.4-1.9 2.6-3.9 3.6-6a.2.2 0 0 0-.1-.3c-1.9-.7-3.8-1.6-5.6-2.6a.2.2 0 0 1 0-.4l1.1-.9a.2.2 0 0 1 .2 0c11.7 5.4 24.4 5.4 36 0a.2.2 0 0 1 .2 0l1.1.9a.2.2 0 0 1 0 .4c-1.8 1-3.6 1.9-5.6 2.6a.2.2 0 0 0-.1.3c1 2.1 2.3 4.1 3.6 6a.2.2 0 0 0 .2.1 58.6 58.6 0 0 0 17.9-9 .2.2 0 0 0 .1-.2c1.5-15.3-2.4-28.6-10.2-40.4a.2.2 0 0 0-.1-.1zM23.7 37.3c-3.5 0-6.4-3.2-6.4-7.2s2.8-7.2 6.4-7.2c3.6 0 6.5 3.3 6.4 7.2 0 4-2.8 7.2-6.4 7.2zm23.7 0c-3.5 0-6.4-3.2-6.4-7.2s2.8-7.2 6.4-7.2c3.6 0 6.5 3.3 6.4 7.2 0 4-2.8 7.2-6.4 7.2z'

const pills: ReadonlyArray<Pill> = [
  ...Array.map(navPages, (page) => ({
    label: Match.value(page.route).pipe(
      Match.when('docs', () => 'Docs'),
      Match.when('community', () => 'Community'),
      Match.when('why-loom', () => 'Why Loom'),
      Match.orElse(() => 'Source'),
    ),
    href: page.href,
    mark: carpetMark(page.carpet, '17px'),
  })),
  {
    label: 'GitHub',
    href: 'https://github.com/athrio-com/loom',
    mark: brandMark('0 0 24 24', '#C4CCE4', GITHUB_PATH),
  },
  {
    label: 'Discord',
    href: 'https://discord.gg/loom',
    mark: brandMark('0 0 71 55', '#7C8CFF', DISCORD_PATH),
  },
]

const pillRow = (focus: number, h: HtmlBuilder<Message>): Html =>
  h.div(
    [h.Class('palette-pills')],
    Array.map(pills, (pill, index) =>
      h.a(
        [
          h.Class(index === Math.min(focus, pills.length - 1) ? 'pill-link here' : 'pill-link'),
          h.Href(pill.href),
          h.Style({ animationDelay: `${40 + index * 34}ms` }),
        ],
        [pill.mark, pill.label],
      ),
    ),
  )

## A page and its passages, drawn

A page's row leads with a tilde, the mark a corpus wears, then its name and the path it answers on. Its passages indent beneath it, each behind a chevron, showing the windowed fragment with the matched words picked out in the page's own colour.

The row holding the focus washes in that colour at low strength, so a reader sees which page they are about to open as well as which row they are on.

=>

const wash = (page: SearchPage, focused: boolean): string =>
  focused ? `rgba(${page.rgb}, 0.15)` : 'transparent'

const passageRow = (
  page: SearchPage,
  passage: Passage,
  query: string,
  focused: boolean,
  h: HtmlBuilder<Message>,
): Html => {
  const fragment = fragmentOf(passage, query)
  return h.button(
    [
      h.Class(focused ? 'palette-row here' : 'palette-row'),
      h.Type('button'),
      h.Style({ background: wash(page, focused) }),
      h.OnClick(WentTo({ route: page.route })),
    ],
    [
      h.span(
        [h.Class('sigil'), h.Style({ color: focused ? page.ink : '#4E5D8C' })],
        ['>'],
      ),
      h.span(
        [h.Class('fragment')],
        [
          fragment.pre,
          h.span([h.Class('palette-hit'), h.Style({ color: page.ink })], [fragment.hit]),
          fragment.post,
        ],
      ),
    ],
  )
}

const groupBlock = (
  group: Group,
  query: string,
  focus: number,
  from: number,
  h: HtmlBuilder<Message>,
): Html =>
  h.div(
    [h.Class('palette-kids-wrap')],
    [
      h.button(
        [
          h.Class('palette-group'),
          h.Type('button'),
          h.Style({ background: wash(group.page, focus === from) }),
          h.OnClick(WentTo({ route: group.page.route })),
        ],
        [
          h.span([h.Class('sigil'), h.Style({ color: group.page.ink })], ['~']),
          h.span([h.Class('name')], [group.page.label]),
          h.span([h.Class('path')], [group.page.path]),
        ],
      ),
      h.div(
        [h.Class('palette-kids')],
        Array.map(group.rows, (passage, index) =>
          passageRow(group.page, passage, query, focus === from + 1 + index, h),
        ),
      ),
    ],
  )

## The palette over the page

The palette stacks four bands: the query, then the pills or the results, and the key hint along the bottom. Which middle band shows depends only on whether anything has been typed and whether the search has settled.

The hint bar and the loading stitch both take the colour of the page the focus is on, so the palette's edges answer to wherever a reader is headed. With nothing typed yet, that page is the first in the list.

=>

const focusedPage = (model: Model): SearchPage => {
  const list = focusables(groupsFor(model.query))
  return pipe(
    focusedIn(list, model.focus),
    Option.map((focusable) => focusable.page),
    Option.getOrElse(() => searchPages[0] as SearchPage),
  )
}

const results = (model: Model, h: HtmlBuilder<Message>): Html => {
  const groups = groupsFor(model.query)
  const starts = pipe(
    groups,
    Array.map((group) => group.rows.length + 1),
    Array.reduce([0] as ReadonlyArray<number>, (acc, size) => [
      ...acc,
      (acc[acc.length - 1] ?? 0) + size,
    ]),
  )
  return h.div(
    [h.Class('palette-body')],
    groups.length === 0
      ? [h.span([h.Class('palette-empty')], [`no match for “${model.query.trim()}”`])]
      : Array.map(groups, (group, index) =>
          groupBlock(group, model.query, model.focus, starts[index] ?? 0, h),
        ),
  )
}

~

While a search is still settling the palette shows a stitched bar drawing itself sideways — the shuttle of a loom, in the colour of the page in focus — with a single word beneath it.

=>

const searching = (page: SearchPage, h: HtmlBuilder<Message>): Html =>
  h.div(
    [h.Style({ display: 'flex', flexDirection: 'column', flex: 'none' })],
    [
      h.span([
        h.Class('palette-stitch'),
        h.AriaHidden(true),
        h.Style({
          background: `repeating-linear-gradient(90deg, ${page.ink} 0 6px, transparent 6px 12px)`,
        }),
      ], []),
      h.span([h.Class('palette-loading')], ['searching']),
    ],
  )

~

The count reads as a sentence rather than a number, so one result does not come out as "1 results".

=>

const countOf = (total: number): string => `${total} ${total === 1 ? 'result' : 'results'}`

const hintBar = (model: Model, page: SearchPage, h: HtmlBuilder<Message>): Html =>
  h.div(
    [
      h.Class('palette-bar'),
      h.Style({
        background: `rgba(${page.rgb}, 0.13)`,
        borderTopColor: `rgba(${page.rgb}, 0.28)`,
        color: page.ink,
      }),
    ],
    [
      h.span([], ['↑↓ move']),
      h.span([], ['⏎ open']),
      h.span([h.Class('count')], [countOf(focusables(groupsFor(model.query)).length)]),
    ],
  )

~

The middle of the card is one of three things, and never two: the pills when nothing is typed, the stitch while the search settles, and the results once it has.

=>

const paletteMiddle = (
  model: Model,
  page: SearchPage,
  h: HtmlBuilder<Message>,
): ReadonlyArray<Html> =>
  Match.value({ typed: model.query.trim() !== '', settling: model.searching }).pipe(
    Match.when({ typed: false }, () => [pillRow(model.focus, h)]),
    Match.when({ settling: true }, () => [searching(page, h)]),
    Match.orElse(() => [results(model, h), hintBar(model, page, h)]),
  )

export const palette = (model: Model, h: HtmlBuilder<Message>): Html => {
  const page = focusedPage(model)
  return h.div(
    [h.Class('palette-shell'), h.Role('dialog')],
    [
      h.div(
        [h.Class('palette-card')],
        [
          h.div(
            [h.Class('palette-head')],
            [
              queryField(model, h),
              h.button(
                [
                  h.Class('palette-esc'),
                  h.Type('button'),
                  h.AriaLabel('Close search'),
                  h.OnClick(ClosedSearch()),
                ],
                [h.span([h.Class('cross')], ['×']), h.span([], ['esc'])],
              ),
            ],
          ),
          ...paletteMiddle(model, page, h),
        ],
      ),
    ],
  )
}

## The footer that ends every page

The footer carries the brand on the left and every link the site offers on the right. The brand is the Loom mark in its cream face, since the ground here is dark, beside the wordmark and the one sentence that says what Loom is.

The links are one flat list, and each is a chip rather than a line of a column. Nine short labels in one run beat three headed groups here: a footer is where a reader goes looking for one particular door, and nine doors side by side are read faster than nine sorted into piles. The order runs from what a reader most often wants to what they rarely do — the documentation first, the organisation last.

=>

type FooterLink = { readonly label: string; readonly href: string; readonly ink: string }

const footerLinks: ReadonlyArray<FooterLink> = [
  { label: 'Docs', href: '/docs', ink: '#6E9BFF' },
  { label: 'Source', href: '/source', ink: '#FF6A3D' },
  { label: 'Why Loom?', href: '/why-loom', ink: '#FFC53D' },
  { label: 'Community', href: '/community', ink: '#A987FF' },
  { label: 'Discord', href: 'https://discord.gg/loom', ink: '#A987FF' },
  { label: 'Github', href: 'https://github.com/athrio-com/loom', ink: '#19D69C' },
  { label: 'License', href: '/community', ink: '#FFC53D' },
  { label: 'Sponsors', href: '/community', ink: '#E8318A' },
  { label: 'About Nonprofit', href: '/community', ink: '#6E9BFF' },
]

export const footer = (h: HtmlBuilder<Message>): Html =>
  h.footer(
    [h.Class('footer')],
    [
      h.div(
        [h.Class('footer-inner')],
        [
          h.div(
            [h.Class('footer-top')],
            [
              h.div(
                [h.Class('footer-brand')],
                [
                  h.div(
                    [h.Class('footer-mark')],
                    [
                      loomMark('dark', 0.78),
                      h.div([h.Class('footer-word')], ['Loom']),
                    ],
                  ),
                  h.div(
                    [h.Class('footer-blurb')],
                    ['Literate programming framework for AI-assisted engineering.'],
                  ),
                ],
              ),
              h.div(
                [h.Class('footer-chips')],
                Array.map(footerLinks, (link) =>
                  h.a(
                    [h.Class('footer-chip'), h.Href(link.href), h.Style({ '--chip-ink': link.ink })],
                    [link.label],
                  ),
                ),
              ),
            ],
          ),
          h.div(
            [h.Class('footer-bottom')],
            [
              h.span([], ['© Athrio Media OÜ 2026']),
            ],
          ),
        ],
      ),
    ],
  )

## The module {Tangle}[chrome.ts]

The module opens with what the site contains — the pages the nav names, the pages the palette searches, and the passages inside them — because every piece of chrome reads from it. Then the two bars and the button they share, the palette built up from its fragments and keys, and the footer.

